(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has had episodes of wheezing, chest tightness and cough for a year, worse at night and with cold air. Her lung exam is normal today. Which test best confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Allergy skin testing","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide","Chest x-ray","Spirometry with a bronchodilator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry is the objective measurement of lung function, with best results when measured before and after a short-acting bronchodilator. Reduced values that improve 12% or more after albuterol strongly support asthma, even when the exam is normal between episodes, as hers is. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 53, 59"},{"q":"A healthy 29-year-old woman has acute histoplasmosis confirmed by urine antigen after clearing out a bat-infested attic. Ten days after onset her fever is resolving and her cough is improving, and she is not short of breath. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Observation without an antifungal","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Amphotericin-based induction","Itraconazole for 12 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severity and host risk guide treatment, and mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult often needs observation rather than antifungals. She is healthy and already improving, so neither itraconazole nor amphotericin is needed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman using home oxygen keeps an electric space heater beside her recliner, where she sits with her tubing. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Lower her flow while the heater runs","A heater is safe because it has no flame","Keep the heater 3 feet away","Keep oxygen at least 10 feet from the heater"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, including electric appliances as well as pilot lights and candles. A space heater beside her recliner is a heat source, so she must keep her oxygen at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"In the CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score, which blood urea nitrogen value earns a point?","choices":["Above 10 mg/dL","Above 20 mg/dL","Above 30 mg/dL","Above 40 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urea above 20 mg/dL (7 mmol/L) earns 1 point; urea means blood urea nitrogen in mg/dL or serum urea in mmol/L. The other points are confusion, respiratory rate of 30 or more, low blood pressure and age 65 or older. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man is newly diagnosed with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 35% predicted. He has never had a pneumococcal vaccine. When should he receive it?","choices":["After his first exacerbation","At age 65","Now, at diagnosis","Once heart disease develops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pneumococcal vaccine is routine at 65, but patients with severe disease (1-second volume below 40%) or cardiac comorbidities should get it earlier, essentially when diagnosed and recognized at that severity. At 35% he qualifies now. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"From what age is pneumococcal vaccination recommended for all adults, whatever their risk conditions?","choices":["19 years","50 years","65 years","75 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All adults aged 50 or older who are conjugate-naive or have an unknown history are vaccinated: one 20- or 21-valent conjugate dose, or a 15-valent dose followed by the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine. Younger adults, 19 to 49, need a qualifying risk condition. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says that, walking on level ground, he has to stop for breath after about 100 meters or after a few minutes. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 3 is stopping for breath after walking about 100 meters or after a few minutes on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms, which places him in group B or E depending on exacerbation risk. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 8 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. She was admitted to the hospital once in the past year for an exacerbation. Using the 2020 four-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group D","Group A","Group C","Group B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 8 and a dyspnea grade of 1 mean fewer symptoms, and 1 exacerbation leading to hospitalization means high risk. In the 2020 scheme, few symptoms with high risk is group C (in the current scheme she would be E). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant and has diabetes tests positive for both influenza and coronavirus disease 2019 on a multiplex test. She has mild symptoms that began yesterday and a normal oxygen saturation. Which antiviral should be avoided because of her pregnancy?","choices":["Molnupiravir","Remdesivir","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Oseltamivir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Molnupiravir is an adult alternative used only when the preferred coronavirus treatments are unsuitable, and it is avoided during pregnancy. Her pregnancy makes it the drug to avoid, while the influenza is treated with oseltamivir, which is preferred in pregnancy. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 104, 108, 120"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man was ventilated for several days with his carbon dioxide held near 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg); the rate was then turned down quickly. The next gas: pH 7.30 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 40 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What best explains this result?","choices":["Post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis","Elevated-gap lactic acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Days of low carbon dioxide let his kidneys excrete bicarbonate (chronic respiratory alkalosis compensation). Rapid correction left that low bicarbonate of 19 unopposed, a post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man on postoperative day 1 after a knee replacement has an oxygen saturation of 89% on room air. He is comfortable, speaking full sentences, with no distress. Which device is the usual first choice?","choices":["Venturi mask at 50%","Simple mask at 10 L/min","Nasal cannula at a low flow","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After knee replacement he is comfortable and speaking full sentences with a saturation of 89% and no distress, so his hypoxia sits at the mild end. The nasal cannula, a low flow device set at 1-6 L/min, is the usual first choice for mild hypoxia and gives 24-44%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 15, 16"},{"q":"Which presentation suggests spinal tuberculosis?","choices":["Painless, persistent node enlargement","Headache with altered mentation","Pleuritic pain with an effusion","Back pain with vertebral destruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spinal tuberculosis causes back pain, vertebral destruction or neurologic deficits. Extrapulmonary disease can involve almost any organ, and organ-specific specimens and urgent assessment may be needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a metabolic alkalosis with a high urine chloride. He takes no diuretics and has not vomited. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which finding should be checked next to narrow the cause?","choices":["His urine chloride again","His expected carbon dioxide by Winter","His blood pressure","His anion gap"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high urine chloride (above 40 mEq/L) means saline-unresponsive alkalosis, whose causes split by blood pressure: normotensive (severe potassium depletion, hypoparathyroidism and hypercalcemia) or hypertensive (primary aldosteronism, mineralocorticoids, licorice). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which compensates more for the same change in carbon dioxide: acute or chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Chronic","Neither; the lungs compensate","They compensate equally","Acute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate rises about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide in acute respiratory acidosis, but about 3.5 mEq/L per 10 in chronic. Renal compensation takes days to complete, so chronic compensates more. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman began hyperventilating at a high-altitude resort this afternoon. Before her kidneys respond, what is the initial compensation for her respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Cells release hydrogen ions for potassium","The kidneys retain bicarbonate","The kidneys excrete bicarbonate","She hypoventilates to retain carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial response to respiratory alkalosis is cellular secretion of hydrogen ions in exchange for potassium. Later the kidneys excrete bicarbonate and retain chloride, lowering bicarbonate, potassium and raising chloride. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has just been prescribed albuterol for newly diagnosed asthma. Which side effects should she be told to expect?","choices":["Insomnia and acid reflux","Oral thrush and hoarseness","Bruising and truncal weight gain","Muscle tremor and palpitations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting beta-2 agonists such as albuterol relax airway smooth muscle for about 3 to 6 hours, and their side effects include muscle tremors and palpitations. Knowing this keeps a new user from stopping her rescue inhaler out of alarm. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with a large lung abscess has not improved after several weeks of beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor therapy, and computed tomography shows no obstructing lesion. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Physical therapy for chest physiotherapy","Pulmonary or infectious disease review","Outpatient follow-up in 6 months","Switch to clindamycin, no referral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large or refractory abscesses need pulmonary or infectious disease review for drainage or surgery. Computed tomography and bronchoscopy are used for obstruction, unusual pathogens or failure to improve, and her abscess is both large and not improving. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman reports severe breathlessness, stopping after about 100 meters on level ground, but her post-bronchodilator forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 82% predicted with a ratio of 0.68. Her heart evaluation is normal. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Reassurance with no follow-up","Long-term oxygen","Triple inhaled therapy","Referral to pulmonology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her dyspnea grade of 3 is far worse than her near-normal airflow (grade 1, 82%) would explain. Symptoms out of proportion to pulmonary function tests, like diagnostic uncertainty, is a listed reason for pulmonology referral. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 34, 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man is recovering in the hospital from an exacerbation. He has been clinically stable for 18 hours, walks across the room, eats and sleeps without waking from breathlessness, and his home oxygen is arranged, but he still needs albuterol every 2 hours. Which finding delays his discharge?","choices":["Home oxygen arranged","Albuterol needed every 2 hours","Stable for 18 hours","Walking across the room"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A discharge criterion is needing inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist no more often than every 4 hours. Every 2 hours means his airflow is not yet controlled enough for home, even though his other criteria are met. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with asthma had facial flushing, red eyes and wheezing several hours after taking aspirin last month. He now has low back pain from lifting. Which medication should he avoid?","choices":["Celecoxib","Albuterol","Meloxicam","Ibuprofen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flushing, conjunctival irritation and wheeze after aspirin, even when delayed up to 12 hours, mark aspirin-sensitive asthma. Ibuprofen is a non-selective anti-inflammatory drug that cross-reacts and must be avoided; selective agents may be used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"Which two airways are listed as artificial airways, as opposed to tracheal airways?","choices":["Venturi mask and trach collar","Nasal cannula and simple mask","Oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal","Endotracheal tube and tracheostomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oropharyngeal airway and the nasopharyngeal airway are the two listed as artificial airways. The tracheal airways, the endotracheal tube and tracheostomy, are listed separately. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which statement about allergen avoidance and the development of asthma is accurate?","choices":["Avoidance does not reduce the risk of developing asthma","Avoidance doubles the risk of atopic dermatitis","Avoidance cures established asthma","Strict avoidance in infancy prevents asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled allergens are common triggers and are implicated in sensitization, yet avoiding them does not reduce the risk of developing asthma; early exposure may even be protective by building tolerance. Avoidance still helps patients who already have asthma by removing triggers. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with asthma notices that her wheezing and chest tightness flare at the same predictable point in every menstrual cycle and are mild the rest of the month. Which type of asthma describes this pattern?","choices":["Occupational asthma","Intrinsic asthma","Catamenial asthma","Aspirin-sensitive asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catamenial asthma is asthma whose symptoms occur at predictable times during the menstrual cycle, as with this woman's monthly flares. It is one of the other types of asthma alongside occupational, cardiac, exercise-induced and aspirin-sensitive patterns. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with advanced human immunodeficiency virus infection has 4 weeks of cough, fever, night sweats and weight loss. His interferon-gamma release assay is negative. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Accept the negative assay as reassuring","Repeat the assay in 8 to 10 weeks","Sputum testing for tuberculosis now","Start latent-infection treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms or high clinical suspicion require evaluation for disease now, with microbiologic testing even when the skin test or assay is negative. Immunosuppression reduces assay sensitivity and can cause false negatives, so sputum specimens go for smear, culture and a rapid molecular test. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with chronic lung disease and a 60-year-old man with pneumonia are both on oxygen. The nurse asks what saturation target to use for each. Which principle applies?","choices":["The target varies with the patient","Both should be kept at 88%","Both should match room air","Both should be kept at 100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Response to oxygen is judged by saturation that improves to a target that varies with the patient. Her chronic lung disease and his pneumonia are different situations, so one number does not fit both, and each target is set for the patient. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man starts treatment for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease and asks how long he will need it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Until his chest imaging is normal","6 months after symptoms resolve","12 months past culture conversion","12 months from the first dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment continues for at least 12 months after culture conversion, so the clock starts when cultures turn negative, not with the first dose. That makes the whole course longer than 12 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden pleuritic chest pain and fast breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); partial pressure of oxygen 68 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100 mm Hg). Which process best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Hyperventilation from a pulmonary embolism","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Diuretic-induced metabolic alkalosis","Hypoventilation from opioid pain medicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 30 is respiratory alkalosis, and her bicarbonate of 23 has barely moved (acute). Pulmonary embolism is a listed pulmonary cause; after surgery, with sudden pleuritic pain and a low oxygen, it fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man admitted with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia is improving on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. On day 3 his blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae that is susceptible to narrower agents. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Narrow to the culture results","Add vancomycin","Switch to cefepime","Continue both for 14 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are reassessed daily and narrowed when possible, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. He is improving and his organism and its susceptibilities are known, so broad empiric coverage is no longer needed; duration follows clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 45"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman who lives in a nursing home is brought in with 2 days of fever and productive cough, and her admission chest radiograph shows a new infiltrate. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated from her airway and has not recently been hospitalized for intravenous antibiotics. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Hospital-acquired pneumonia","Community-acquired pneumonia","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Health care-associated pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired pneumonia is acquired outside the hospital or present early in admission, and prior health care contact does not exclude it. The health care-associated category is retired, and nursing-home residence alone does not mandate broad-spectrum therapy; her lack of resistant-organism risks keeps her on the community pathway. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing chronic bronchitis-type disease develops distended neck veins that are most prominent during expiration and swelling of both ankles. His blood gas shows chronic hypoxemia. Which complication do these findings suggest?","choices":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Cor pulmonale","Theophylline toxicity","Pneumothorax"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cor pulmonale, right heart strain or failure, is a secondary complication of this disease, seen with the chronic bronchitis picture of cyanosis and edema. Distended jugular veins, particularly during expiration, and edema fit it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 17, 21"},{"q":"What does fractional exhaled nitric oxide testing identify in asthma?","choices":["Bronchodilator reversibility","Allergen triggers","Airway inflammation","Hyperinflation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fractional exhaled nitric oxide testing identifies airway inflammation. It sits with the chest radiograph and allergy testing among the other tests, while allergy skin or blood testing identifies the allergen triggers. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has 4 weeks of fevers, sweats, fatigue and a cough with foul-smelling purulent sputum. A chest radiograph shows a cavity with an air-fluid level. Which initial therapy is most appropriate?","choices":["Immediate surgical resection","Clindamycin as first choice","Intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam","Routine chest physiotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cavity with an air-fluid level after weeks of fever and foul sputum is a lung abscess, which usually begins with antibiotics. A beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor is the usual first choice, such as intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate, often for several weeks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 56"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia has a PSI (Pneumonia Severity Index) total of 104 points. Which risk class does that total fall in?","choices":["Class II","Class III","Class IV","Class V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On the interpretation table, 91 to 130 points is Class IV, with a mortality of 9.3%. The index plus clinical judgment is the preferred support for the admission decision, and a total just over 100 already carries roughly a 1 in 10 risk of death. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 30, 31"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is starting doxycycline for Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. She asks what to expect and how to avoid getting it again. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["A vaccine can prevent another episode","The cough may persist for a while","Return if the cough lasts past day 3","Amoxicillin is a good backup drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Counseling for Mycoplasma infection explains that the cough may persist, and that she should return for shortness of breath or deterioration. No vaccine is available, so avoiding another episode depends on general measures, not immunization. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has vomited repeatedly for three days and is dizzy on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 91 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.50 is alkalemia, and the bicarbonate of 35 is high and matches it, so it is metabolic. The carbon dioxide of 47 is respiratory compensation (about 0.75 per 1 mEq/L rise). Low potassium and chloride with orthostatic dizziness fit vomiting, which loses hydrochloric acid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 19, 39, 40, 42, 59"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has asthma symptoms throughout every day, uses his rescue inhaler several times a day, wakes with symptoms nearly every night, and is extremely limited in daily activities. His forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 52% predicted. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms throughout the day, rescue use several times a day, night awakenings often 7 times a week, extreme limitation and a 1-second volume below 60% predicted define severe persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chest pain has a myocardial infarction with ST elevation. He is alert, with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Give a Venturi mask at 28%","Withhold oxygen at this saturation","Give a nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Give a non-rebreather at 15 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is alert with a saturation of 96% on room air, well above the 91% threshold, and oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. With no hypoxia, no oxygen is the right plan. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11, 15, 16"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has persistent asthma symptoms and frequent rescue inhaler use despite a prescribed inhaled corticosteroid plus long-acting beta-2 agonist. She has not been seen since the prescription 6 months ago. Which step should come first?","choices":["Refer for a monoclonal antibody","Test for steroid-resistant asthma","Check adherence and inhaler technique","Start long-term oral corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and follow-up visits review medications, compliance and correct use before adjusting therapy. Her increased rescue use signals poor control, so adherence and technique are checked first. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 43, 90, 91"},{"q":"Which electrolyte change may accompany chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Hypochloremia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperchloremia","Hypernatremia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride, so serum chloride may fall (hypochloremia) while bicarbonate rises. Chloride leaves with the acid the kidney excretes. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man ventilated for 6 days in the intensive care unit after cardiac surgery develops ventilator-associated pneumonia. Which organisms are most likely responsible?","choices":["Pseudomonas or multidrug-resistant organisms","Mycoplasma pneumoniae or Chlamydophila","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Histoplasma or other endemic fungi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ventilator-associated pneumonia is more likely to be Pseudomonas or multidrug-resistant pathogens, and hospital and ventilator pneumonia are driven by Staphylococcus aureus and gram-negative bacilli, including Pseudomonas and Enterobacterales. It carries higher mortality and a longer intensive care stay. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 39, 48"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has newly diagnosed drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. Which regimen is the standard treatment?","choices":["Rifampin alone for 4 months","Two drugs for 2 months, then four for 4","Isoniazid alone for 9 months","Four drugs for 2 months, then two for 4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The standard drug-susceptible regimen is isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for the initial 2 months, then isoniazid and rifampin for 4 months, given daily with directly observed or video-observed therapy to support completion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with asthma, already on an inhaled corticosteroid and a long-acting beta-2 agonist, has heart disease and worries about side effects. Her clinician is considering adding tiotropium. Which property of tiotropium is reassuring for her?","choices":["Little systemic absorption","A strong anti-inflammatory effect","Fast onset that suits rescue use","Greater effect than beta-2 agonists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tiotropium, an anticholinergic, has little or no systemic absorption and therefore few side effects. It inhibits only the cholinergic reflex part of bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion, and it is less effective than beta-2 agonists with a slow onset. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection is admitted with moderate Pneumocystis pneumonia confirmed on induced sputum. He has no drug allergies and normal kidney function. Which treatment is preferred?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Atovaquone","Intravenous pentamidine","Clindamycin plus primaquine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the preferred treatment for Pneumocystis pneumonia, given intravenously for moderate-to-severe disease and switched to oral therapy after improvement, for 21 days in disease linked to human immunodeficiency virus. Nothing in his history, no allergy and no kidney problem, moves him to an alternative. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with septic shock has a lactate elevation. Sodium 132 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 96 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is her anion gap?","choices":["24","28","36","48"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = 132 - (96 + 12) = 132 - 108 = 24, above 9 to 15: elevated. Lactic acidosis, the L of the mnemonic, raises the gap through unmeasured anions. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease develops sudden sharp chest pain and severe breathlessness while coughing. Breath sounds are absent over the right chest. Before treating this as an exacerbation, which test best excludes the diagnosis most likely here?","choices":["Spirometry with a bronchodilator","Chest x-ray","6-minute walk test","Alpha-1 antitrypsin level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pneumothorax is one of the diagnoses to exclude before calling a decline an exacerbation, and the chest x-ray, though not a diagnostic standard for the disease itself, can rule out other diagnoses such as heart failure or pneumothorax. Sudden pain with absent breath sounds points there. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 56"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 93%, but during a walking test she drops to 85%. She is mobile and reports exertional breathlessness. Which oxygen plan is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen just at night","Oxygen over 15 hours a day","No oxygen for now","Oxygen during exertion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe exertional desaturation, 88% or less with exertion, qualifies for long-term oxygen during exertion. Her resting saturation of 93% is moderate desaturation, without a resting indication, so oxygen is used with activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a severe asthma attack is tiring, and her carbon dioxide has risen over an hour to 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate rise of about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L","31 mEq/L","34 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide rose 20 (40 to 60); acutely bicarbonate rises about 1 per 10, so 24 + 2 = 26 mEq/L. Asthma is a listed cause of acute respiratory acidosis, and a near-normal bicarbonate marks an acute process. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection has mild Pneumocystis pneumonia. He had a severe rash with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole in the past, so clindamycin plus primaquine is planned. Which test should be checked before primaquine is given?","choices":["Serum beta-D-glucan","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Serum lactate dehydrogenase","Pulmonary function testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His past reaction removes trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and clindamycin plus primaquine is a severity-based alternative. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is checked before primaquine or dapsone, because a deficiency makes those drugs unsafe. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has had a chronic cough with sputum for months. Her sputum acid-fast bacilli smear is positive. What does this smear result establish?","choices":["Pulmonary tuberculosis disease","Nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease","Latent tuberculosis infection","Acid-fast organisms, species not yet known"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, and bronchiectasis raises the risk of nontuberculous disease, so the species still has to be identified. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 5"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with no known tuberculosis risk factors has his tuberculin skin test read at 48 hours. There is 20 mm of erythema around 9 mm of transverse induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Negative; 9 mm is below his 15 mm cut-off","Positive; 9 mm is above a 5 mm cut-off","Positive; 20 mm is above a 10 mm cut-off","Positive; 20 mm is above his 15 mm cut-off"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The reader measures transverse induration in millimeters and does not measure erythema. With no known risk factors his cut-off is 15 mm, and his induration is 9 mm, so the result is negative despite the wide redness. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with no immune problems received the updated 2026-2027 coronavirus disease 2019 vaccine in September. He asks whether he needs anything more this season. Which plan is appropriate?","choices":["Monthly doses through spring","No further dose this season","A second dose in 4 weeks","A second dose about 6 months later"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An updated 2026-2027 vaccine is recommended for adults 18 or older, and adults 65 or older generally need two seasonal doses about 6 months apart. At 72 he needs a second dose about 6 months after September. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with heart disease has a severe metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.56 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 49 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 43 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does her alkalemia affect oxygen delivery to her tissues?","choices":["It shifts the curve to the right","It decreases delivery","It does not change delivery","It increases delivery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen and delivery to the tissues decreases. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which group is at high risk for severe influenza?","choices":["Healthy adults aged 50 to 64","Recent international travelers","Healthy children aged 6 to 12","Adults aged 65 or older"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-risk groups include adults 65 or older, children younger than 5 (especially younger than 2), residents of long-term care facilities, immunocompromised patients, pregnancy and the first 2 weeks postpartum, severe obesity and chronic organ disease. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 98"},{"q":"Which lung volume change reflects air trapping in this disease?","choices":["Reduced total lung capacity","Reduced residual volume","Raised residual volume","Reduced functional residual capacity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Air trapping raises the residual volume, and hyperinflation raises total lung capacity and functional residual capacity. With exertion, dynamic hyperinflation worsens and drives dyspnea. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The clinical definition of chronic bronchitis requires chronic cough and sputum production for at least how long?","choices":["1 month a year for 1 year","3 months a year for 2 consecutive years","6 months a year for 3 consecutive years","12 months a year for 5 consecutive years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic bronchitis is a clinical definition: chronic cough and sputum production typically for at least 3 months a year for 2 consecutive years. Emphysema, by contrast, is defined anatomically, by destruction and enlargement of the alveoli. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with stable, long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a serum bicarbonate of 33 and an arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 54 mmHg on a blood gas. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Primary metabolic acidosis","Laboratory error to be repeated","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Compensated chronic hypercapnia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blood gases in this disease usually show chronic hypercapnia, with arterial carbon dioxide above 45 mmHg, and the metabolic panel may show a raised bicarbonate. The combination shows a compensatory metabolic alkalosis, as his 54 and 33 do. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is a carbon dioxide above the Winter prediction in metabolic acidosis a warning sign?","choices":["It confirms an elevated anion gap","It means the kidneys are compensating","It means the acidosis is resolving","It can signal impending respiratory failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A carbon dioxide higher than the Winter formula predicts means metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis. This can be a sign of impending respiratory failure, so it is a warning. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman in a severe asthma exacerbation sits leaning forward with hunched shoulders and uses her accessory muscles. Loud wheezing earlier has faded, and now she has only decreased breath sounds with a prolonged expiration. What does the loss of wheezing most likely indicate?","choices":["Resolution of the exacerbation","Severe airflow obstruction","Vocal fold dysfunction","A response to her inhaler"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Wheezing may not be present in severe exacerbations, which may show only decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. With her accessory muscle use and posture, a quiet chest signals worse obstruction, not improvement. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has had a cough for 10 days that began with a cold. Her sputum is green, and she asks for an antibiotic. Her temperature, heart rate, respiratory rate and oxygen saturation are normal, and her lungs have scattered rhonchi that clear with coughing and no focal findings. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Supportive care, no antibiotic","Levofloxacin","Chest radiograph and sputum culture","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal vital signs, no focal lung findings and rhonchi that clear with a cough describe uncomplicated acute bronchitis, usually a self-limited viral illness. Green or yellow sputum does not separate viral from bacterial infection, so no antibiotic is given; explain the expected course and agree on symptom goals. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 18, 19, 22"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with asthma follows a written action plan. His personal best peak flow is 600 L/min. Today he is very short of breath, his quick-relief medicine has not helped, and his peak flow is 260 L/min. Which action-plan zone is he in?","choices":["Yellow zone","Between zones, so recheck tomorrow","Red zone","Green zone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A peak flow of 260 out of a personal best of 600 is about 43%, below 50% of personal best, and he is very short of breath despite quick-relief medicine. Those are red-zone, medical-alert findings: take the rescue medicine and call the doctor or get help now. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"}]};
